    "updated_at": "2026-11-22T17:33:42Z",
    "body": "Facts for S3 from the S2 kernel (PR #3532, #2627). This comment records them. It does not fix them.\n\n- The shared device of `camcraft-gpu` (`shared_device`) is not the render device of the UI. The cut buffer `scene.cut` is made on the render device (`crates/ui/src/trace.rs:197-203`). The S3 caller has two options:\n  - make its own `CutKernel::new(render_device)` and run `CutKernel::run` on the render device and queue;\n  - or use `cut_or_cpu` on the shared device and upload the result with `write_cut` (a read-back and an upload).\n- The kernel writes u32 ordered keys during the cut (`atomicMax`). The third pass `cs_decode` turns each key into the bits of its f32 in the same buffer. After the pass, `trace.wgsl` can read the buffer as `array<f32>`, in the `pack_cut` layout. A kernel that writes into `scene.cut` directly needs `STORAGE` usage on that buffer (it has it) and a bind group that the kernel owns.\n- The input is plain numbers. `camcraft_sim::stock_sim::twin_items` gives `TwinItem { tile, base, lo, hi }`; S3 maps it to `camcraft_gpu::CutItem` field by field.\n- `CutKernel::run` blocks for at most `CUT_WAIT` (10 s). S3 must call it off the UI thread.\n- The render device has no `on_uncaptured_error` handler that this kernel installs. The kernel uses error scopes on the calling thread.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
    "pin": null,
